<h1>Connectors</h1>
<p class="fine">Where the estate's numbers can come from besides a file somebody
sends. A connector fetches what the provider already publishes and normalises it
into the same columns the engine reads; it never computes a figure and never
dates a row it cannot date. Facts here were checked against each provider's own
documentation on 2026-08-17.</p>

<section class="panel">
  <h2>The rule that decides which one to use</h2>
  <p class="fine">An export delivered to your own storage is paid for once, as
  storage. The same data pulled through a cost API is charged per call:
  <b>Cost Explorer is USD 0.01 per request</b>, <b>CloudWatch GetMetricData is
  billed per thousand metrics requested</b> and is outside the free tier, and
  <b>BigQuery is USD 6.25 per TiB scanned</b>. So the drop is the default here,
  and anything metered states its price and will not run without a confirmation.
  Nothing on this page contacts a provider by itself.</p>
</section>


<section class="panel">
  <h2>Cost and usage</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>connector</th><th>how</th><th>status</th><th>what it costs to run</th></tr>
    
    <tr>
      <td><b><a href="/connectors/aws-data-exports">AWS Data Exports (FOCUS 1.2)</a></b>
        
        
        
        <div class="fine">aws &middot; AWS ships FOCUS 1.0 and FOCUS 1.2 tables only. There is no 1.3 or 1.4 table, so a claim that this feeds FOCUS 1.4 would be false. Delivery is at least daily; the previous period can still be revised for about two weeks after month end. Historical backfill up to 36 months is not self-service: it needs a support case.</div>
        <div class="fine">auth: none for the reader - it reads files already delivered to a folder</div>
      </td>
      <td><span class="chip">export-drop</span></td>
      <td><span class="chip st-in-progress">built</span>
          </td>
      <td><span class="chip st-done">no per-run charge</span>
        <div class="fine">No charge for the export itself: you pay S3 storage and requests for the delivered objects, pennies a month at this size. Creating the export is a one-off, and the CreateExport API rejects SELECT *, so the query must name every column.</div>
        <div class="fine"><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cur/latest/userguide/what-is-data-exports.html" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">provider documentation</a></div>
      </td>
    </tr>
    
    <tr>
      <td><b><a href="/connectors/azure-focus-export">Azure Cost Management export (FocusCost)</a></b>
        
        
        
        <div class="fine">azure &middot; definition.type FocusCost combines actual and amortized cost in one schema. Not available at management-group scope or on MOSP billing accounts. Data lands within about 4 hours of a run, but a new export can take 24 hours for its first delivery, and the month-close reconciliation run can trail 72 hours after the month ends. The Execute API can pull up to 7 years of history where the portal stops at 13 months.</div>
        <div class="fine">auth: none for the reader - it reads files already delivered to a folder</div>
      </td>
      <td><span class="chip">export-drop</span></td>
      <td><span class="chip st-in-progress">built</span>
          </td>
      <td><span class="chip st-done">no per-run charge</span>
        <div class="fine">Cost Management and its APIs are free to Azure customers; you pay only Blob storage for the delivered files.</div>
        <div class="fine"><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/tutorial-improved-exports" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">provider documentation</a></div>
      </td>
    </tr>
    
    <tr>
      <td><b><a href="/connectors/gcp-focus-bigquery">GCP billing export, FOCUS table in BigQuery</a></b>
        
        
        
        <div class="fine">gcp &middot; The FOCUS export is Preview, hosted by Google in an immutable dataset with a 2-year TTL and no storage charge to you. Enabling it is Console-only: there is no gcloud command, REST endpoint or Terraform resource for the toggle. Backfill reaches the start of the previous month for multi-region datasets and nothing at all for single-region. Google omits several standard FOCUS columns and substitutes x_ ones.</div>
        <div class="fine">auth: service account with bigquery.dataViewer AND bigquery.jobUser</div>
      </td>
      <td><span class="chip">metered-api</span></td>
      <td><span class="chip st-in-progress">built</span>
          </td>
      <td><span class="chip st-blocked">metered</span>
        <div class="fine">BigQuery on-demand querying is USD 6.25 per TiB scanned, first 1 TiB per month free, minimum 10 MB billed per table referenced. One monthly pull of a FOCUS table at this size is well inside the free tier; a dashboard that re-queries per page view is not. The reader takes a JSON dump from `bq query --format=json`, so the query is run once, deliberately, and never on a page render.</div>
        <div class="fine"><a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/export-data-bigquery-tables/focus-export" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">provider documentation</a></div>
      </td>
    </tr>
    
    <tr>
      <td><b><a href="/connectors/aws-cost-explorer">AWS Cost Explorer (GetCostAndUsage)</a></b>
        
        
        
        <div class="fine">aws &middot; Capped at two GroupBy dimensions per call against a full FOCUS row, so it is a bootstrap for history, not a replacement for the export. Data refreshes at least every 24 hours.</div>
        <div class="fine">auth: IAM principal with ce:GetCostAndUsage</div>
      </td>
      <td><span class="chip">metered-api</span></td>
      <td><span class="chip st-in-progress">built</span>
          </td>
      <td><span class="chip st-blocked">metered</span>
        <div class="fine">USD 0.01 per paginated request, and 0.01 x N for a consolidated view built from N source accounts. A daily job is cents a month; a loop over dimensions is not. Hourly resource-level data costs extra per usage record and is kept only 14 days.</div>
        <div class="fine"><a href="https://docs.aws.amazon.com/aws-cost-management/latest/APIReference/API_GetCostAndUsage.html" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">provider documentation</a></div>
      </td>
    </tr>
    
    <tr>
      <td><b><a href="/connectors/azure-cost-query">Azure Cost Management Query API</a></b>
        
        
        
        <div class="fine">azure</div>
        <div class="fine">auth: Entra ID principal with Cost Management Reader on the scope</div>
      </td>
      <td><span class="chip">free-api</span></td>
      <td><span class="chip st-in-progress">built</span>
          </td>
      <td><span class="chip st-done">no per-run charge</span>
        <div class="fine">No charge, but throttled in Query Processing Units: 12 QPU per 10s, 60 per minute, 600 per hour per tenant, and roughly one QPU per month of data queried. Microsoft&#39;s own advice is to call no more than once a day, because the data refreshes every four hours anyway.</div>
        <div class="fine"><a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/costs/manage-automation" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">provider documentation</a></div>
      </td>
    </tr>
    
  </table>
</section>

<section class="panel">
  <h2>Kubernetes</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>connector</th><th>how</th><th>status</th><th>what it costs to run</th></tr>
    
    <tr>
      <td><b><a href="/connectors/kubecost-allocation">Kubecost Allocation API</a></b>
        
        
        
        <div class="fine">kubernetes &middot; GET /model/allocation on port 9090, window is required, aggregate takes namespace, controller, label:&lt;name&gt; and more. The response carries cpuEfficiency and ramEfficiency, which is the point: efficiency is utilisation, and no billing export contains it. __idle__ and __unallocated__ come back as line items and are kept as such.</div>
        <div class="fine">auth: none on self-hosted: the REST API has no built-in authentication. Reach it inside the cluster, through kubectl port-forward, or behind an authenticating ingress - never exposed openly.</div>
      </td>
      <td><span class="chip">free-api</span></td>
      <td><span class="chip st-in-progress">built</span>
          </td>
      <td><span class="chip st-done">no per-run charge</span>
        <div class="fine">Free. The Foundations tier is free for up to 250 cores but keeps only 15 days of metrics, which is the real constraint: a monthly close needs the pull to happen before the window rolls off.</div>
        <div class="fine"><a href="https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/kubecost/self-hosted/3.x?topic=apis-allocation-api" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">provider documentation</a></div>
      </td>
    </tr>
    
    <tr>
      <td><b><a href="/connectors/opencost-allocation">OpenCost Allocation API</a></b>
        
        
        
        <div class="fine">kubernetes &middot; GET /allocation on port 9003, not /model/allocation on 9090, and it always computes live from Prometheus rather than serving a precomputed set. Narrower parameter surface: no filters, no format, no sharing options. OpenCost is in the FinOps Foundation&#39;s FOCUS adopter list; Kubecost is not.</div>
        <div class="fine">auth: none - reach it in-cluster or via port-forward</div>
      </td>
      <td><span class="chip">free-api</span></td>
      <td><span class="chip st-in-progress">built</span>
          </td>
      <td><span class="chip st-done">no per-run charge</span>
        <div class="fine">Free and open source; the CNCF engine Kubecost is built on.</div>
        <div class="fine"><a href="https://opencost.io/docs/integrations/api/" rel="noreferrer noopener" target="_blank">provider documentation</a></div>
      </td>
    </tr>
    
  </table>
</section>

